# Striatum Workflow Router

You are driving a Striatum workflow. Striatum is a local-first
runner whose live state is `.striatum/state.sqlite3` in the target
repository. The runner — not your prose — advances state.

## When to invoke

- The operator asked to drive a workflow against a target repo.
- The operator mentioned "Striatum", `striatum`, "claim a packet",
  or a job/run/lease/work-packet vocabulary.
- A workflow file (typically `workflow.json`) is on disk.

## Authoritative reference

Run `striatum --help` to see the top-level verb set on this
machine. Each work packet's `commands` block contains the exact
follow-up commands you should call (`ack`, `heartbeat`,
`publish-artifact`, `block`, `verdict` / `submit-review`,
`complete`); use those literally.

## Where to look next

- **`striatum-scaffold`** — initialize a new workflow / start a run.
- **`striatum-claim-loop`** — the actual work loop
  (register-session → claim-next → ack → publish-artifact →
  complete).
- **`striatum-supervise`** — long-lived supervised agent sessions.
- **`striatum-recover`** — `status`, `why`, `doctor`, recovery
  flows.

